[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: The FRRouting BGP daemon (bgpd) on router2 has failed.
- Mechanism: With bgpd down on router2, the eBGP peering over 193.10.11.0/24 cannot establish. Router1 therefore learns no route to 200.1.1.0/24 and responds to pc1’s probes with ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable” from 195.11.14.1.
- Localization: Physical link between router1:eth0 and router2:eth0 is up (ethtool reports “Link detected: yes” on both sides). Router1’s FRR is running and shows its neighbor 193.10.11.2 stuck in “Active” state. On router2, the FRR service is failed and bgpd is not running:
  • router2 systemctl: “frr.service … Active: failed (failed)”
  • router2 vtysh: “bgpd is not running”
  • router1 vtysh: neighbor 193.10.11.2 in Active, 0 prefixes.
  • router1 routing table lacks any route to 200.1.1.0/24; hence pc1->pc2 pings get “From 195.11.14.1 Destination Net Unreachable.”
- Root cause: The bgpd process (FRR service) on router2 stopped/failed, dropping the eBGP session and preventing advertisement of 200.1.1.0/24 to router1.
- Corrective fix: Restart the FRR service on router2 to bring bgpd back and reestablish eBGP:
  • On router2: systemctl restart frr
  • Verify: vtysh -c "show ip bgp summary" should show Established with prefixes received; router1’s route table should then contain 200.1.1.0/24 and pc1 should regain reachability to pc2.
No recabling is needed; the inter-AS link is up at Layer 1/2. The issue is a routing daemon failure on router2. 
=== END ===